course: dictionary
lesson: 6489
title: Ne: a tiny word that means not
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: You may meet «ne» in French lessons, on a map, or in a sentence from another language. It is not an English word on its own, so look at the words around it to find out which job it is doing.
explain: In French, «ne» is the first half of the way to say «not». «Je ne sais pas» means «I don't know». The «ne» goes before the verb, and «pas» goes after it, so the two words work as a pair.
explain: On a compass or a map, NE is short for north-east, the direction halfway between north and east. «The wind blew from the NE» tells you the wind came from that direction.
explain: Say «ne» like «nuh». In everyday French people often drop the «ne» and say «je sais pas», but in lessons keep both parts so you learn the full pattern.
fact: «Ne» is French, and it is not an English word on its own.
fact: «Ne … pas» means not, as in «je n'aime pas» (I don't like).
fact: «Ne … jamais» means never, «ne … plus» means no more, and «ne … rien» means nothing.
fact: «NE» on a map is short for north-east.
fact: In another language, «ne tak» means «not like that» or «wrong», and «tak» means yes.
exercise: «Je ne sais pas.» What does this mean? | I know it well | Yes, I know it | I know everything | * I don't know
exercise: «The wind blew from the NE.» What does NE stand for? | * North-east | South-west | North-south | North-west
exercise: In «je n'aime pas», which word comes after the verb? | je | * pas | n' | aime
test: In «ne … pas», what does the pair mean? | again | very | yes | * not
test: Why is NE shown on a compass? | It is a sound in English | It means yes | * It is a direction, halfway between north and east | It is a French word for hello
exam: Is «ne» a common English word on its own? | Only in Scotland | Yes, it means yes | Yes, it is used in every English sentence | * No, it is French, or a short form like NE on a map
